<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bay of Bourgneuf</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bay of Bourgneuf is an inlet of the Atlantic on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> estuary, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pays-de-retz">Pays de Retz</a> and the island of Noirmoutier. Its salt marshes produced large quantities of sea salt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and Hanseatic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fleets came each year to load this 'bay salt' for northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bay-of-bourgneuf.jpg" alt="Bay of Bourgneuf" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kev22 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beauvoir-sur-Mer_-_Port_du_Bec_-_Vue_sur_la_baie_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
